# Originals, signatures and contested submissions

Keep an evidence inventory with source, acquisition date, original filename, relevant fact and limits. Use `documented`, `reported by the user`, `inference` and `disputed` as working distinctions, without forcing those labels into every sentence of the final answer. A later copy can prove its own content without proving what was sent earlier.

## Digital files

Preserve originals; extract or convert into new files. A PDF conversion helps reading but does not replace the original signed envelope. Two files with the same name can differ. A hash comparison establishes byte identity or difference, not substantive correctness, upload time or legal admissibility.

For a .p7m or other signed object, distinguish:

1. Successful content extraction/rendering.
2. Mathematical signature integrity and its actual verification result.
3. Certificate chain, trusted identity, validity/revocation status and the relevant validation time.
4. A signing-time field versus a trusted timestamp or another legally relevant time reference.
5. Evidence linking those exact bytes or their authenticated content to the original submission.

Do not infer trusted legal dating from the time displayed by a signing application. Do not infer successful original submission from possession of a correctly signed document. Conversely, a technical retrieval failure is not proof that the original was blank. Explain precisely which proposition each test supports.

A newly signed envelope may have different bytes while containing the same underlying document. Compare the extracted payload and, if needed, its substantive content before claiming the text changed. Preserve both envelopes and the comparison method. Hashes alone cannot establish whether an authority received either envelope.

## PEC and application portals

Seek the original message, its actual attachments, certified transport data, available receipts and relevant protocol/application identifiers. Determine which evidence proves acceptance, delivery, portal acquisition or only later availability. Link each attachment to the correct message/submission rather than a folder listing. Request a copy of what the authority actually acquired and inspected when the dispute turns on that difference.

Do not equate a screenshot of signature verification with a certified upload receipt. Do not overwrite the original application with a later resubmission. Preserve any integration request and compare exactly what it asked for with the response.

## Conversations and reasons

Record who spoke, when, and what the user reports was said. Separate contemporaneous notes, sent written summaries, and any written acknowledgement. Do not invent exact quotes or treat uncontradicted correspondence as automatic acceptance. Do not attribute bad faith or a software fault without evidence.

Read the actual written reasons, including incorporated documents. If the user says the reasons are unclear, examine that as a possible argument without converting it into an assertion that no reasons exist. Distinguish evidence of contradictory conduct from the legal consequence sought.

Technical/legal source leads: [AgID on qualified signatures](https://www.agid.gov.it/it/piattaforme/firma-elettronica-qualificata), [AgID on PEC](https://www.agid.gov.it/it/piattaforme/posta-elettronica-certificata), [EUR-Lex](https://eur-lex.europa.eu/) for applicable eIDAS provisions and [Normattiva](https://www.normattiva.it/) for applicable CAD rules. Open the relevant text before asserting a legal effect; the links themselves are not a validation result.
